 is $1.$ This occurs when $x\\to -\\infty .$ However, note  that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $1$ and $\\infty ,$ but not including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $1<y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as \n$1<f(x)<\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9c/9c69a1ff6845f4be53f42e166a682baf804919ccba0f344ccdd38bc249584efc.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2eb98296-341b-5be8-a509-1dce3cf14c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489201 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-3.$ This occurs when $x=1.$\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs when $x\\to -\\infty .$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-3$ and $0,$ including $-3$ but not including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-3\\le y<0,$ which can also be written as  $-3\\le f(x)<0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489211 #edb/ref 1069 "q-235999" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489211 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218648